Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Vent Five Reaches the Ridge
- Story & Context: The debut runs at 132 BPM. Lucia begins on a low fujara fundamental held across four bars while Darius uses sparse clipped kicks and a short root sub. During RISE she increases breath pressure and the flute jumps into brighter overtone regions. The drums become more syncopated, but the sub is progressively shortened rather than enlarged. Zuzana adds one prepared-celesta fifth at the midpoint of the climb. Marek plays one deep drum hit every two bars while the phrase remains low, then stops completely at RIDGE. Noel enters with a muted bass-trumpet fourth during the transition upward but drops out at the brightest overtone. For eight bars the track feels fastest and most intense despite containing almost no bass below the kick. During DESCENT the sub and low drum return in stages. The final fujara note ends on the same low fundamental where the track began.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Long galvanized ventilation shaft marked VENT 5 rising through several warehouse floors, with four labeled bands FLOOR, RISE, RIDGE, DESCENT and a single fujara silhouette stretching vertically beside them. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “vent five,” “higher air,” “hold the ridge,” and “weight comes back below.”

Track 2: Storage Temperature Climbs While the Bass Disappears
- Story & Context: At 136 BPM, Darius programs the EP’s hardest Baltimore club pattern: chopped kick clusters, dry claps, and brief triplet stomps. Lucia plays shorter repeated fujara breaths that each climb only partway up the overtone ladder. Every new breath starts lower than the previous one but reaches a slightly brighter RIDGE. Zuzana answers each RIDGE with one dry celesta octave. Marek replaces selected electronic kicks only during FLOOR states. Noel sustains a muted bass-trumpet pedal through the first three climbs, then removes it entirely for the highest one. The central trick is low-frequency subtraction: each successive ascent loses more sub until the final RIDGE is carried almost entirely by clap, celesta, breath noise, and upper harmonics. When the low fundamental returns, one single sub block feels larger than the entire previous minute.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old analog cold-storage temperature dial rising while a second printed bass-level scale falls in the opposite direction, wooden apple crates and fujara finger holes in shallow focus. Spoken fragments limited to “temperature up,” “bass down,” “same room,” and “one block returns.”

Track 3: Orchard Door Opens Below the Last Harmonic
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 128 BPM and begins with the longest continuous breath on the EP. Lucia holds a low fujara tone, rises slowly through several harmonic states, remains at RIDGE for nearly eight bars, then descends. Darius uses only kick, clap, and one narrow stomp figure, leaving large vacancies around each register jump. Zuzana plays no celesta until the DESCENT. Marek waits until Lucia drops below the upper overtone region, then enters with a single deep drum hit. Noel plays two quiet bass-trumpet falls that mirror the downward movement without copying exact pitches. During the last sixteen bars the club drums simplify while the flute keeps descending. A real insulated storage door is opened in the room after the final high harmonic has passed, introducing a short rush of cold corridor air into the room microphones. The last sound is the low fujara fundamental with no electronic sub underneath it.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Heavy insulated orchard-storage door opening into a dark ventilation corridor, one hand-drawn harmonic ladder descending toward the floor, cold vapor moving beneath stacked fruit crates. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “door below,” “last harmonic,” “air comes through,” and “leave the floor acoustic.”

